From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 13:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] shared/tinyusb: stall the CDC IN endpoint, with the USB
 interrupt held off, before clearing its TX FIFO on disconnect.

On an ESP32-S3 on its native USB, a machine.USBDevice disconnect while
the board is still sending over the built-in CDC either reboots it
("Guru Meditation Error: Core 1 panic'ed (Interrupt wdt timeout on
CPU1)") or hangs it for good, with no REPL on either port
(micropython-pydevices#70). Both active(False) and a switch from the
built-in device to a runtime one (active(True) while connected) do it.

mp_usbd_disconnect() cleared the CDC TX FIFO and then stalled the CDC IN
endpoint. With TinyUSB 0.21 (patch 0014) the DWC2 driver runs in slave
mode and pulls each packet of a CDC transfer straight from that FIFO in
its TX-FIFO-empty interrupt, so this is the same failure patch 0024 fixed
in the line-state callback: emptied mid-transfer, the interrupt writes
nothing, stays asserted and re-enters until the watchdog fires. A coredump
from the unpatched build shows it interrupting edpt_disable() under
usbd_edpt_stall(), called from mp_usbd_disconnect().

Stalling first isn't enough on its own. Disabling a busy IN endpoint
polls DIEPINT for NAK-effective and then endpoint-disabled, and the
interrupt handler writes back every DIEPINT bit it reads, so a
TX-FIFO-empty interrupt that lands between the hardware setting a flag
and the poll reading it clears the flag, and the poll spins forever:
that is the hang. With the stall moved ahead of the clear, the panics
stopped but the hangs went to 11 in 40.

So the CDC IN stall now runs with the USB interrupt held off, in an atomic
section, and the clear comes after it. When the section ends, the pending
interrupt refills the disabled endpoint's hardware FIFO from the TX FIFO,
which still holds the data; that FIFO then never drains, so the interrupt
stops pulling, and the clear is safe. The stall of a runtime IN endpoint
that is busy has the same polling race and is left as it was: the OUT
side of that loop waits on a flag only the interrupt's RX FIFO handling
produces, so it can't simply run with the interrupt off.

Proven on a Waveshare ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-7 (ESP32_GENERIC_S3 LCD_7,
native USB CDC on Windows), driven from its UART console while a host
reads the CDC port. Each trial checks the host is receiving, prints 1000
characters and disconnects at once or 50 to 300 us later, then counts
resets and hangs on the UART:

  | path                                 | before                     | this patch |
  |--------------------------------------|----------------------------|------------|
  | active(False) mid-print              | 13/40 (8 panics, 5 hangs)  | 0/40       |
  | switch to a runtime device mid-print | 7/20 (4 panics, 3 hangs)   | 0/40       |

Stalling first without the atomic section took active(False) to 0
panics and 11 hangs in 40.

With the patch, the CDC came back after every reconnect, and the friendly
REPL, a 14 KB paste-mode block, 4000 lines of output read back whole and
in order, and mpftp (exec, a 58 KB put with hash, run) all worked.

The ESP32-S2 and ESP32-P4 build TinyUSB 0.21 the same way, in slave mode
(CFG_TUD_DWC2_DMA_ENABLE defaults to 0), so they share the path.

Drop this patch when upstream reorders the disconnect or TinyUSB makes
tud_cdc_n_write_clear() and endpoint disable safe under an in-flight
transfer.

Based-on: micropython 0fd6c573e (tag v1.29.0)
---
 shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c | 17 +++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c b/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c
--- a/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c
+++ b/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c
@@ -484,10 +484,23 @@
     }
 
     #if MICROPY_HW_USB_CDC
-    // Ensure no pending static CDC writes, as these can cause TinyUSB to crash
-    tud_cdc_write_clear();
     // Prevent cdc write flush from initiating any new transfers while disconnecting
+    //
+    // This comes before the clear below, and with the USB interrupt held off.
+    // In slave mode the DWC2 driver (ESP32-S2/S3/P4) pulls each packet of an
+    // IN transfer straight from the CDC TX FIFO in its interrupt: emptied
+    // mid-transfer, the TX-FIFO-empty interrupt finds nothing to write, stays
+    // asserted, and re-enters until the interrupt watchdog resets the chip.
+    // And disabling a busy IN endpoint polls for the NAK-effective and
+    // endpoint-disabled flags, which that same interrupt clears if it runs in
+    // between, leaving the poll spinning for good.
+    mp_uint_t atomic_state = MICROPY_BEGIN_ATOMIC_SECTION();
     usbd_edpt_stall(RHPORT, USBD_CDC_EP_IN);
+    MICROPY_END_ATOMIC_SECTION(atomic_state);
+    // Ensure no pending static CDC writes, as these can cause TinyUSB to crash.
+    // The disabled endpoint's FIFO no longer drains, so the interrupt has
+    // stopped pulling from this one by now.
+    tud_cdc_write_clear();
     #endif
 
     bool was_connected = tud_connected();
